    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1083">

                <pb facs="1370" n="1360" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1360"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102/4–1746</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of Slate
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-04-17T10:00:00+07:00">April 17, 1946—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>693. Settlements and Diplomatic Quarter. British Embassy here has been authorized
                    by London to agree to suggestions mentioned in our 619, April 3 to Dept
                    (repeated Shanghai 244) and has so informed Chinese Foreign Office. (Sent Dept
                    693, repeated Shanghai 296, April 17, 10 a.m.)</p>
                <p>Dr. Wang Hua-cheng of Foreign Office informed me yesterday that suggestions were
                    sent to Executive Yuan few days ago and approval was expected shortly whereupon
                    Foreign Office will request Embassy to designate American advisers on
                    Liquidation Commissions.</p>
                <p>Authorization and information requested in our 619, April 3, would be
                    appreciated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
